plain_text:
In NGINX config, what will the upstream request URI be given this location:
location /test {
  proxy_pass <URL>:$port/;
}

And the request URI: <URL>

Certainly! Here's the equivalent code in JavaScript to remove URLs from a set of strings using regular expressions:

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

javascriptCopy codeconst strings = [
  "Hello, check out this website: <URL>",
  "I found an interesting article at <URL>",
  "Click here: <URL> for more information"
];

const pattern = /https?:\/\/\S+/g;

const cleanedStrings = strings.map((string) => string.replace(pattern, ''));

console.log(cleanedStrings);

Output:

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Copy code[
  "Hello, check out this website: ",
  "I found an interesting article at ",
  "Click here:  for more information"
]

In this JavaScript code, we define the regular expression pattern/https?:\/\/\S+/g, which matches HTTP or HTTPS URLs followed by any non-whitespace characters. We then use themap()function to iterate over each string in thestringsarray and usereplace()to replace the matched URLs with an empty string, effectively removing them. The resulting cleaned strings are stored in thecleanedStringsarray, which is logged to the console.

Solved with brute force but it's giving TLE. public int maxProfit(int[] prices) {

        int maxProfit = 0;

        for(int i = 0;i<prices.length - 1;i++) {
            for(int j = i+1; j < prices.length; j++){
                int profit = prices[j] - prices[i];
                maxProfit = profit > maxProfit ? profit : maxProfit;
            }
        }
        return maxProfit;
    }
